 152: ## Decisions
 153: 
 154: ### A. The tenant-read rule ships as ONE module, and the acceptance suite drives it
 155: 
 156: `supabase/functions/_shared/visibility.ts`, under the same two constraints `memberships.ts` states
 157: of itself: zero non-relative imports, no Deno global, no I/O, no clock. It is compiled by
 158: `tests/at/tsconfig.json` (strict, `skipLibCheck: false`, no DOM) and run by Deno in the edge
 159: runtime.
 160: 
 161: It holds exactly three things:
 162: 
 163: - `tenantReadAllowed(viewer, scope)` - the one rule. An NGO account reads an organisation's
 164:   non-public data only where it holds a membership row IN THAT organisation. A volunteer reads a
 165:   project's non-public data only where it is THAT project's assigned volunteer. A platform admin
 166:   reads both, for every account. A logged-out viewer reads neither.
 167: - `publicProjectView(project, organization)` - what a project shows the world. One statement of
 168:   what is public, so "public" is a value this repository can point at rather than a habit.
 169: - `TENANT_NOT_FOUND` - see decision B.
 170: 
 171: It fails closed on every value it does not recognise, the posture `parseOrgRole` states and gives
 172: its reasons for. The decision is the shared module's; the edge functions consult it; the loop-tier
 173: adapter delegates to it, so a loop green is a claim about shipped code.
 174: 
 175: **Why the argument shape matters.** `tenantReadAllowed` takes the viewer's membership IN THE TARGET
 176: organisation, never a list of the viewer's memberships. An implementation that authorised from the
 177: caller's standing in some other organisation cannot express itself through the signature at all -
 178: the same device `orgAdminActionAllowed` uses, and the same reason.
 179: 
 180: ### B. "No existence oracle" becomes STRUCTURAL, not a rule somebody applies
 181: 
 182: The criterion's clause is that a denial must not reveal whether the thing exists. That is a
 183: property of two answers being IDENTICAL, so the module exports the answer itself:
 184: 
 185: ```
 186: TENANT_NOT_FOUND = { status: 404, body: { ok: false, reason: '<one sentence>' } }
 187: ```
 188: 
 189: Every non-public read surface returns exactly this constant for BOTH "no such row" and "exists,
 190: not yours". There is nowhere in the surface to put a second refusal, so the two cannot drift apart
 191: by an edit that looks harmless.
 192: 
 193: **THE TARGET ROW IS READ LAST, AND THAT ORDERING IS THE SECOND STRUCTURAL CLAUSE (gate-1 ruling
 194: 4).** Every read a decision needs - the caller's account, its membership in the target organisation,
 195: its assignment to the target project - is issued BEFORE the target row is read, and the target read
 196: is the LAST read the handler makes. The decision is then computed from values already in hand. The
 197: reason is that an outage answer must not depend on whether the target exists: with a lookup AFTER
 198: the target read, a fault reachable only on the existing-row path answers 502 for a real foreign id
 199: while a nonexistent id already answered 404, and those two answers are an existence oracle outside
 200: the constant. With nothing after the target read, a fault answers the same 502 either way, by
 201: construction rather than by care.
 202: 
 203: **How the two answers are compared, per tier (gate-1 ruling 5).** At integration tier the
 204: comparison is on the RAW response text and the status, through the unparsed helper of step 9 - not
 205: through `callFunction`, which returns `{ status, json }` after `JSON.parse` (`_live.ts:266-288`)
 206: and would let two differently serialised bodies compare equal. At loop tier there are no bytes, so
 207: the comparison is deep equality of the returned outcome value and its status. Both assert equality
 208: of the two responses, never merely that both refused.
 209: 
 210: **404 rather than 403, and the alternative was considered.** `update-organization` uses one 403 for
 211: both cases and is equally non-oracular; either works so long as it is the SAME. 404 is chosen
 212: because it carries no information at all, while a 403 states that something is there to be
 213: forbidden. The cost is that a 404 for a row that does exist is, strictly, not true - and that cost
 214: is the point of the criterion.
 215: 
 216: **What is NOT claimed: timing.** Response time is a side channel this item does not measure and
 217: does not defend. The merge ruling states it.
 218: 
 219: **The outage case is neither.** A read that FAILED - the database unreachable - answers 502 and
 220: says so, the shape `update-organization` lines 101-106 already use, so an outage can never be read

