 223: ### C. Enforcement lands in the database, not only in the function
 224: 
 225: The policy set uses two stable `security definer` helpers keyed on `auth.uid()` -
 226: `public.viewer_is_org_member(uuid)` and `public.viewer_is_platform_admin()` - so a policy on
 227: `org_memberships` does not recurse into itself. Then `select` policies on `organizations`,
 228: `org_memberships`, `acknowledgments` and `projects`, and `grant select ... to authenticated` on
 229: each. **Nothing is granted to `anon`**: the public surface is an edge function, not a table.
 230: 
 231: **THE SET LANDS IN TWO MIGRATIONS, ONE PER SLICE (gate-1 ruling 7).** Several permissive `select`
 232: policies on one table are OR'd, so the set splits by BRANCH without either half becoming vacuous.
 233: Slice 1 ships `viewer_is_org_member` and the organisation-member policies, which AT-001.21's own
 234: Data API control exercises. Slice 2 ships `viewer_is_platform_admin`, the assigned-volunteer policy
 235: and the platform-admin policies, which AT-001.23 and AT-001.40 exercise in the same slice that
 236: ships them. The rule this obeys: **a slice does not ship a policy branch it does not test.** In
 237: slice 1 the volunteer and the platform admin are denied at the Data API because no policy admits
 238: them yet, which is what slice 1's denials assert; slice 2 re-runs AT-001.21 and .22, so a new
 239: branch that broke a denial fails there.
 240: 
 241: **THE HELPERS' POSTURE, DICTATED (gate-1 ruling 6).** Each helper is `language sql`, `stable`,
 242: `security definer`, `set search_path = ''`, with every name inside it schema-qualified - the
 243: posture all four existing migrations use. Then `revoke execute on function <full signature> from
 244: public;` and **`grant execute on function <full signature> to authenticated, service_role;`**. The
 245: grant to `authenticated` is mandatory and is the part that copying the existing service-role-only
 246: posture would break: a policy expression is evaluated as the querying role, so the querying role
 247: needs EXECUTE on any helper the policy calls. The comment states that, and states why the resulting
 248: remote-procedure exposure leaks nothing - both helpers answer only about `auth.uid()`, so a caller
 249: learns only its own standing, which it already knows.
 250: 
 251: **Why both layers.** The Data API is literally the criterion's "direct API/ID probing", and with a
 252: policy a denied keyed read returns `[]` - the same answer as a row that does not exist, so the
 253: no-oracle property is free there. The edge function is the path a UI must use (`CLAUDE.md`: the UI
 254: never touches the database directly) and it needs decision B to get the same property. A green on
 255: one arm alone would leave the other open.
 256: 
 257: ### D. Three read surfaces, each traceable to a named clause
 258: 
 259: | function | `verify_jwt` | clause it serves |
 260: |---|---|---|
 261: | `organization-dashboard` | true | AT-001.21 "NGO A's non-public data ... dashboard" |
 262: | `project-workspace` | true | AT-001.22 "that project's non-public data", AT-001.23 "that project's working data" |
 263: | `public-project-page` | **false** | AT-001.22 "the public project page remains visible", AT-001.24 "public surfaces (listings, project pages)" |
 264: 
 265: `public-project-page` is the first `verify_jwt = false` block in this repository and the config
 266: comment that says all functions are authenticated is corrected in the same change. It reveals that
 267: a project exists, deliberately: that is the criterion's own "beyond public surfaces" carve-out, and
 268: keeping it a separate function is what stops the public answer contaminating the no-oracle test.
 269: 
 270: None of the three is a general reader. Each returns one projection, the way
 271: `update-organization` refuses to be a general profile editor.
 272: 
 273: ### E. What this green covers, said before it is built
 274: 
 275: The criteria enumerate drafts, ledger, files, thread, tasks and dashboard. **Only the dashboard
 276: kind exists** - an organisation, its projects, its membership and its acknowledgments. The others
 277: are surfaces no requirement has landed. This item isolates every kind of tenant data that exists
 278: and states the rest as absent, in the plan, in the per-id table and in the merge ruling.
 279: 
 280: **And it makes the absence self-correcting.** A conformance arm at integration tier reads the live
 281: catalog. The declared lists are shipped code, so a table added by a later requirement fails the
 282: build until somebody decides which it is. This is the device the manifest already ratifies for
 283: D6.L2's write routes, applied to reads. Without it this leaf's green would be a statement about
 284: today's four tables and nothing else.
 285: 
 286: **WHAT THE ARM CHECKS, DICTATED (gate-1 ruling 8).** "Carries a select policy" is not isolation - a
 287: table with `USING (true)` would satisfy it while exposing every row. The arm checks three things:
 288: 
 289: 1. Every table in `public` appears in **exactly one** of two shipped lists,
 290:    `unreachableByClientRoles` or `tenantIsolated`. A table in neither, or in both, FAILS.
 291: 2. A table in `unreachableByClientRoles` is unreachable for a stated reason: **either**
 292:    `information_schema.role_table_grants` shows no `select` grant to `anon` or `authenticated`,
 293:    **or** row-level security is on and `pg_policies` shows zero `select` policies reaching those
 294:    roles. Both arms are needed. `public.accounts` carries `grant select, insert ... to
 295:    authenticated` and reaches no row only because it has no policy;
 296:    `public.volunteer_profiles` carries `revoke all` and is unreachable by privilege. An arm that
 297:    tested grants alone would call `accounts` reachable and fail on the first run.
 298: 3. A table in `tenantIsolated` carries at least one `select` policy for `authenticated`; no
 299:    `select` policy on it has a `qual` of `true`; and every `select` policy's `qual` names
 300:    `viewer_is_org_member`, `viewer_is_platform_admin`, or that table's declared tenant key column.
 301: 
 302: **What the arm does NOT prove, and the merge ruling says so:** that a declared predicate is
 303: correct. It proves a table is declared, reachable only as declared, and not trivially open.
 304: 
 305: ### F. AT-001.24's UI clause cannot be built here - the decision lands, the screens do not
 306: 
 307: The criterion is about a browser: public surfaces render, authenticated surfaces redirect to
 308: sign-in. `src/` is Lovable territory and CI fails a pull request that touches both territories
 309: (fact 6). There are also no screens to guard (fact 5).
 310: 
 311: So this item lands the three halves it can:
 312: 
 313: 1. the logged-out denial on every non-public API surface - 401 from both authenticated functions,
 314:    and a privilege refusal from the Data API, both measured;
 315: 2. the public surface answering an anonymous caller with the public projection and nothing else;
 316: 3. a shipped route registry naming each route under `src/routes/` as public or authenticated, with
 317:    a conformance arm that fails when a route is not declared. It reads `src/routes/` and
 318:    `src/routeTree.gen.ts` and writes neither - the same out-of-band shape `_source-scan.ts` uses
 319:    for AT-001.17.
 320: 
 321: At integration tier AT-001.24 REFUSES with `CapabilityPending(['ui.logged-out-surface-rendering'])`
 322: - the shape AT-001.05 and AT-001.10 already use. A green there would claim a rendering nobody
 323: observed.
 324: 
 325: **The line between a green id and a capability-pending one (gate-1 ruling 1).** If the criterion's
 326: OUTCOME can be observed without a screen, the id goes green and registers with `{ surface: 'ui' }`,
 327: so a wiring leaf's `--wired` re-run selects it when screens land. If the outcome IS the screen, the
 328: id refuses with a capability. AT-001.21's outcome is "access is denied and nothing leaks",
 329: observable at the API; AT-001.24's outcome is the rendering itself. So AT-001.21 and AT-001.22 are
 330: green AND `ui`-tagged, and AT-001.24 is not green at integration tier.
 331: 
 332: **THE BATCH PARTNER'S CLOSES-LINE IS CONDITIONAL (gate-1 ruling 3).** The merge ruling adds the one
 333: sanctioned `Closes` line for the partner item **only if** the founder has answered open question 1
 334: - by ratifying a D5 wiring leaf for the screens, the way D2 has one, or by ruling AT-001.24's
 335: browser half out of that item. With no founder answer, the line is OMITTED, the partner item stays
 336: open, and the merge ruling states why. This item does not edit `loop/decomp/req-001.md`; filing a
 337: wiring leaf takes founder approval.
 338: 
 339: **This is the open question for the founder. See the last section.**
 340: 
 341: ### G. Every denial body carries a positive control
 342: 
 343: A surface that refused everybody would satisfy AT-001.21, .22 and .24 completely while proving
 344: nothing. Every body therefore performs the ALLOWED read first and asserts it succeeded, so each
 345: refusal is attributable to the tenant boundary rather than to the surface being shut. This is the
 346: discipline every landed body in this suite already applies.
 347: 
 348: ### H. Givens come from the operator, and the plan says so
 349: 
 350: No product path creates a project or assigns a volunteer (`_contract.ts` says so of
 351: `createProjectAsOperator` and `assignVolunteerAsOperator`). The two-organisation Given uses
 352: `createOrganizationAsOperator` and `grantMembershipAsOperator`, which exist. The platform admin
 353: uses `provisionPlatformAdmin`, which exists. **No new operator authority is invented** - every
 354: Given this item needs is already on the surface. Only READ members are added.
