  14:  * .12, .13 and .14 with the session-and-reset one, AT-001.16, .36 and .37 with the per-organisation
  15:  * roles one, AT-001.17 and .32 with the single-seat one, AT-001.19, .39 and .20 with the
  16:  * acknowledgment-identity one, and AT-001.21 and .22 with the tenant-isolation denial one. The
  17:  * other 14 are declared, not faked: each one throws, loudly, stamped with its own id and with the
  18:  * manifest leaf that will make it real.
  19:  *
  20:  * THEY CANNOT SILENTLY GO GREEN. `AtPending` is a thrown error, so the id is RED, and
  21:  * `tests/at/expected/req-001.json` declares that red by SHAPE — `expected.ts` rebuilds the anchored
  22:  * prefix `AtPending: <id> PENDING [sut-missing] — ` from the declaration and compares it from
  23:  * position 0. A red of any other cause fails the declaration instead of hiding inside it, and a red
  24:  * that turns green fails it too, so a later leaf must update the ledger in the same change that
  25:  * lands its id. That file is REQ-001's live progress ledger from this item onward.
  26:  *
  27:  * WHAT THE PREFIX DOES NOT CHECK, said plainly because it is the reason every leaf writes a ledger
  28:  * of its own (`loop/items/AI4DEV-57/pending-ledger.txt`, then
  29:  * `loop/items/AI4DEV-58/pending-ledger.txt`, then `loop/items/AI4DEV-59/pending-ledger.txt`, then
  30:  * `loop/items/AI4DEV-60/pending-ledger.txt`, then `loop/items/AI4DEV-62/pending-ledger.txt`, then
  31:  * `loop/items/AI4DEV-65/pending-ledger.txt`): the
  32:  * tail after the em dash is FREE. `expected.ts`
  33:  * anchors on the prefix only, so a stub whose detail read "todo" would pass every command in this
  34:  * repository. Nothing mechanical holds the detail below to the truth. The check that does is a
  35:  * written one — every leaf named here appears in `loop/decomp/req-001.md`, and the ledger is the
  36:  * artifact a reviewer compares against the manifest.
  37:  */
  38: 
  39: import { AtPending, type AtContext } from './_bind.ts';
  40: 
  41: /**
  42:  * The manifest's leaves, in the manifest's own words shortened to a recall hint. The keys are the
  43:  * deliverable-and-leaf labels from `loop/decomp/req-001.md`; the letters there are the MANIFEST's
  44:  * deliverables and are not the plan's decision letters, which use the same alphabet for a different
  45:  * purpose.
  46:  */
  47: export const LEAF = {
  48:   // SEVEN LABELS ARE GONE FROM THIS MAP rather than kept for symmetry, and the rule is the same for
  49:   // all seven: a leaf label sitting here with nothing pointing at it is a claim that something is
  50:   // still pending when nothing is. `tests/at/expected/req-001.json` declares the landed ids green
  51:   // in the same change each time.
  52:   //   D1.L2 — GitHub OAuth signup and the mandatory GitHub link — landed AT-001.02, .04 and .05.
  53:   //   D2.L1 — email verification and the unverified-write gate on Discovery messages — landed
  54:   //           AT-001.09 and .10, its only two ids.
  55:   //   D2.L2 — session expiry and revocation, auto-refresh, password reset, wrong-password
  56:   //           rejection — landed AT-001.38, .12, .13 and .14, its only four ids.
  57:   //   D3.L1 — per-NGO admin/member roles and multi-NGO membership isolation — landed AT-001.16,
  58:   //           .36 and .37, its only three ids.
  59:   //   D3.L2 — the single-seat NGO and the single-dev project invariant — landed AT-001.17 and .32,
  60:   //           its only two ids, so it is removed here by the item that rides beside D3.L1's.
  61:   //   D4.L1 — acknowledgment identity capture, name, title and authority attestation — landed
  62:   //           AT-001.19, .39 and .20, its only three ids.
  63:   //   D5.L1 — the cross-NGO denial and the unassigned volunteer's denial — landed AT-001.21 and
  64:   //           .22, its only two ids. D5.L2 still points at this map, and it is the leaf D5.L1
  65:   //           unblocks.
  66:   D3_L3: 'D3.L3 (the cross-surface single-seat integration)',
  67:   D5_L2: 'D5.L2 (assigned-volunteer scope, platform-admin reach, logged-out visibility)',
  68:   D6_L1: 'D6.L1 (contact transfer, lost-access recovery and the escalation contact)',
  69:   D6_L2: 'D6.L2 (the lifecycle gate every write route registers through)',
  70:   D6_L3: 'D6.L3 (the append-only audit for role changes and transfer, and sign-in rate limiting)',
  71: } as const;
  72: 
  73: export type LeafLabel = (typeof LEAF)[keyof typeof LEAF];
  74: 
  75: /**
  76:  * One not-yet-landed id's whole body.
  77:  *
  78:  * `sut-missing` rather than `harness-missing` is the honest phase word: the harness is up — this
  79:  * suite's written ids run against it — and what is absent is the system under test for these
  80:  * criteria. The detail is what makes that word honest, by naming WHICH system under test.
  81:  *
  82:  * (The sentence above used to say "this suite's own four ids", which was true when four were
  83:  * written and false from the next leaf onward. The count lives in this file's header and in the
  84:  * ledger, and stating it a second time here only created somewhere for it to drift.)
  85:  */
  86: export function notLanded(leaf: LeafLabel): (ctx: AtContext) => Promise<void> {
  87:   return async (ctx: AtContext): Promise<void> => {
  88:     throw new AtPending(ctx.atId, 'sut-missing', `REQ-001 ${leaf} has not landed`);
  89:   };
  90: }

===== tests/at/suites/req-001/d-tenant-isolation.test.ts =====
   1: /**
   2:  * AT-REQ-001 section E — tenant isolation and visibility.
   3:  *
   4:  * TWO OF THIS FILE'S FIVE IDS ARE NOW WRITTEN — AT-001.21 and AT-001.22, the two DENIALS. Both
   5:  * arrive with `loop/decomp/req-001.md` D5.L1: one organisation cannot reach another's data, and a
   6:  * volunteer who is not assigned to a project cannot reach that project's. AT-001.23, AT-001.40 and
   7:  * AT-001.24 belong to D5.L2 and stay declared here.
   8:  *
   9:  * (This header used to describe the tree as the first accounts leaf left it: row-level security on
  10:  * every table with ZERO policies, which denies everybody. That was the safe default and never the
  11:  * requirement. It stopped being true on 2026-08-12, when this leaf's migration landed the first
  12:  * policy set, so the paragraph is rewritten rather than kept as a statement that now reads wrong.)
  13:  *
  14:  * WHAT A GREEN HERE CLAIMS, said before the bodies rather than after. It claims READ isolation over
  15:  * every kind of tenant data this tree HOLDS — an organisation, its seat, its projects and its
  16:  * acknowledgments — through BOTH paths the criterion names: the edge surface a user interface must
  17:  * use, and direct identifier probing at the Data API with the caller's own access token. It claims
  18:  * that a denial and an absence are the SAME answer.
  19:  *
  20:  * WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM. The criterion's browser route: no screen exists, and `src/` is another
  21:  * territory that CI forbids this pull request to touch. The `ui` surface tag is what enrols the id
  22:  * in a wiring leaf's `--wired` re-run for the day the screens land (gate-1 ruling 1). It claims
  23:  * nothing about drafts, a ledger, files or a thread — no such table exists anywhere in this tree —
  24:  * and nothing about timing side channels.
  25:  */
  26: 
  27: import { expect } from 'vitest';
  28: import { atTest } from './_bind.ts';
  29: // The INTEGRATION-tier procedures. Same criterion, same id, one registration; only the procedure
  30: // differs — at that tier the Given is real rows on a real database, the action is the deployed
  31: // function, and the two refusals are compared as RAW RESPONSE TEXT. See _integration.ts.
  32: import { at00121, at00122, INTEGRATION_TIMEOUT_MS } from './_integration.ts';
  33: import { LEAF, notLanded } from './_pending.ts';
  34: // THE SHIPPED AUTHORITY STATEMENT, imported rather than restated — every completion below is a
  35: // Given and must succeed, and the deployed validation accepts no other attestation.
  36: import { ACKNOWLEDGMENT_IDENTITY_COPY } from '../../../../supabase/functions/_shared/acknowledgment-copy.ts';
  37: 
  38: /** The version string of the ToS + Platform Promise text these bodies accept on the user's behalf. */
  39: const TEXT_VERSION = 'tos-2026-01+promise-2026-01';
  40: /** The address the acknowledgment records — the gateway chain's reported one, never a verified one. */
  41: const CLIENT_IP = '203.0.113.7';
  42: /** The password every email/password registration in this file uses. */
  43: const PASSWORD = 'correct horse battery staple';
  44: /**
  45:  * AT-001.19's three fields, carried by every completion here.
  46:  *
  47:  * Every completion in this file is a GIVEN and must succeed, so each one carries the identity the
  48:  * shared validation requires. Nothing here grades those fields.
  49:  */
  50: const SIGNER = {
  51:   signerName: 'Dana Okonkwo',
  52:   signerTitle: 'Executive Director',
  53:   authorityAttestation: ACKNOWLEDGMENT_IDENTITY_COPY.authorityStatement,
  54: } as const;
  55: 
  56: /**
  57:  * A WELL-FORMED IDENTIFIER THAT NAMES NOTHING — the other half of the no-existence-oracle test.
  58:  *
  59:  * WELL-FORMED IS THE LOAD-BEARING WORD. A malformed value would be refused by the identifier parser
  60:  * long before any tenant rule ran, and the two answers would then differ for a reason that has
  61:  * nothing to do with the criterion. This is a valid uuid that no row in any table carries.
  62:  */
  63: const ABSENT_ID = '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000021';
  64: 
  65: atTest(
  66:   'AT-001.21',
  67:   'one NGO cannot reach another NGO non-public data by UI or by direct id probing',
  68:   // THE `ui` TAG IS GATE-1 RULING 1. The criterion names a browser route this pull request cannot
  69:   // build — `src/` is the other territory and CI fails a diff that crosses both — so the id is
  70:   // graded where its OUTCOME is observable, at the API, and the tag enrols it in a wiring leaf's
  71:   // `--wired` re-run for the day the screens land. `--wired` exits 3 today, so the tag changes no
  72:   // current run.
  73:   //
  74:   // THE RAISED BUDGET IS FOR THE INTEGRATION TIER ONLY. That body registers two accounts, waits for
  75:   // two confirmation messages and signs in twice against a real GoTrue; vitest's own 30 seconds
  76:   // would end it red however correct it was. The loop tier keeps vitest's value.
  77:   { surface: 'ui', timeoutMs: { integration: INTEGRATION_TIMEOUT_MS } },
  78:   {
  79:     default: async ({ open }) => {
  80:       const { w, sut } = await open();
  81:       const NAME_A = 'Riverside Shelter 21A';
  82:       const NAME_B = 'Northgate Foodbank 21B';
  83: 
  84:       // NGO A AND NGO B BOTH COME FROM THE PRODUCT PATH. Each completion writes its organisation and
  85:       // seats its own caller as that organisation's admin, inside one step, so both standings are
  86:       // product facts rather than something this test arranged.
  87:       const sessionA = await sut.registerWithEmailPassword(w.email('ngo-a-21'), PASSWORD);
  88:       const a = await sut.completeSignup(
  89:         sessionA,
  90:         { accountType: 'ngo', organizationName: NAME_A, acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION, ...SIGNER },
  91:         CLIENT_IP,
  92:       );
  93:       expect(a, 'NGO A could not complete signup, so there is no tenant for B to be denied').toMatchObject({ ok: true });
  94:       if (!a.ok || a.organizationId === null) return;
  95: 
  96:       const sessionB = await sut.registerWithEmailPassword(w.email('ngo-b-21'), PASSWORD);
  97:       const b = await sut.completeSignup(
  98:         sessionB,
  99:         { accountType: 'ngo', organizationName: NAME_B, acknowledgmentTextVersion: TEXT_VERSION, ...SIGNER },
 100:         CLIENT_IP,
 101:       );
 102:       expect(b, 'NGO B could not complete signup, so there is no second tenant to do the probing').toMatchObject({ ok: true });
 103:       if (!b.ok || b.organizationId === null) return;
 104: 
 105:       // A PROJECT IN A, so `public.projects` holds a row that belongs to A. The operator provisions
 106:       // it because no product path creates a project at either tier.
 107:       const project = await sut.createProjectAsOperator(a.organizationId, 'Riverside Shelter Website 21');
 108: 
 109:       // A'S ROWS REALLY EXIST, read back with operator authority. Without this the empty Data API
 110:       // answers below would be indistinguishable from four empty tables, and every denial would be
 111:       // measuring the tree's emptiness instead of the policy set.
 112:       expect(await sut.membership(a.organizationId, a.accountId), 'A holds no seat in its own organisation').toMatchObject({
 113:         role: 'admin',
 114:       });
 115:       expect(await sut.projectAssignment(project.id), 'A\'s project is not there').toMatchObject({
 116:         organizationId: a.organizationId,
 117:       });
 118:       expect((await sut.acknowledgments(a.accountId)).length, 'A\'s completion recorded no acknowledgment').toBeGreaterThan(0);
 119: 
 120:       // (1) THE CONTROL — A READS ITS OWN DASHBOARD. A surface that refused everybody would satisfy
 121:       // every denial below while proving nothing at all.
 122:       const own = await sut.organizationDashboard(sessionA, a.organizationId);
 123:       expect(own.ok, 'A was refused its OWN dashboard, so the refusals below are not about the tenant boundary').toBe(true);
 124:       if (!own.ok) return;
 125:       expect(own.status, 'A\'s own dashboard did not answer 200').toBe(200);
 126:       expect(own.value.organizationId, 'A\'s dashboard names a different organisation').toBe(a.organizationId);
 127:       expect(own.value.seat, 'A\'s dashboard does not show A\'s own seat').toMatchObject({ accountId: a.accountId, role: 'admin' });
 128:       expect(
 129:         own.value.projects.map((row) => row.projectId),
 130:         'A\'s dashboard does not show A\'s project, so the projection carries no tenant data to leak',
 131:       ).toEqual([project.id]);
 132: 
 133:       // (2) B IS REFUSED A'S DASHBOARD.
 134:       const foreign = await sut.organizationDashboard(sessionB, a.organizationId);
 135:       expect(foreign.ok, 'NGO B read NGO A\'s dashboard').toBe(false);
 136:       if (foreign.ok) return;
 137: 
 138:       // (3) AND THE SAME CALLER PROBING AN IDENTIFIER THAT NAMES NOTHING RECEIVES THE IDENTICAL
 139:       // ANSWER. This is the no-existence-oracle clause, and it is an EQUALITY rather than two
 140:       // refusals: two answers that both said no while differing in status, or in one field, would
 141:       // still tell B which organisation identifiers are real.
 142:       const absent = await sut.organizationDashboard(sessionB, ABSENT_ID);
 143:       expect(absent.ok, 'an organisation that does not exist answered with a projection').toBe(false);
 144:       if (absent.ok) return;
 145:       expect(absent.status, 'the two refusals carry different statuses, which tells B which identifier is real').toBe(foreign.status);
 146:       // AT THE LOOP TIER THERE ARE NO BYTES, so the comparison is deep equality of the whole returned
 147:       // value. The integration body compares the RAW response text instead — gate-1 ruling 5 — and
 148:       // neither comparison ever crosses the two tiers.
 149:       expect(absent, 'the refusal for a real foreign organisation differs from the refusal for one that does not exist').toEqual(
 150:         foreign,
 151:       );
 152: 
 153:       // (4) THE SAME PAIR THROUGH THE DATA API, on every table that holds tenant data. This is the
 154:       // criterion's "direct API/ID probing": the caller's own access token, PostgREST and the policy
 155:       // set, not the edge surface wearing another name. A denied keyed read answers `[]`, which is
 156:       // byte for byte what a row that does not exist answers — so the no-oracle property is free
 157:       // here, and the pair is asserted anyway because "free" is a claim about today's mechanism.
 158:       const probes = [
 159:         { table: 'organizations' as const, keyedBy: 'id' as const, real: a.organizationId },
 160:         { table: 'org_memberships' as const, keyedBy: 'org_id' as const, real: a.organizationId },
 161:         { table: 'projects' as const, keyedBy: 'org_id' as const, real: a.organizationId },
 162:         { table: 'acknowledgments' as const, keyedBy: 'account_id' as const, real: a.accountId },
 163:       ];
 164:       for (const probe of probes) {
 165:         const keyed = await sut.dataApiRead(sessionB, { table: probe.table, keyedBy: probe.keyedBy, value: probe.real });
 166:         expect(keyed.rows, `B's keyed probe of ${probe.table} was refused before any row was considered`).not.toBeNull();
 167:         expect(keyed.rows ?? [], `B read A's rows out of ${probe.table} by identifier`).toEqual([]);
 168: 
 169:         const nothing = await sut.dataApiRead(sessionB, { table: probe.table, keyedBy: probe.keyedBy, value: ABSENT_ID });
 170:         expect(nothing, `on ${probe.table} a real foreign identifier and one that names nothing answered differently`).toEqual(keyed);
 171:       }
 172: 
 173:       // (5) THE POSITIVE CONTROL AT THE DATA API, and it is what settles that a policy ran at all
 174:       // (gate-1 ruling 9). Without it an empty array from a denied read could be a gateway refusal
 175:       // or a missing table privilege wearing the policy's clothes.
 176:       const ownRow = await sut.dataApiRead(sessionA, { table: 'organizations', keyedBy: 'id', value: a.organizationId });
 177:       expect(ownRow.rows, 'A\'s own keyed read was refused before any row was considered').not.toBeNull();
 178:       expect(ownRow.rows ?? [], 'A\'s own keyed read did not return exactly one row').toHaveLength(1);
 179:       expect((ownRow.rows ?? [])[0], 'A\'s own keyed read returned a different organisation').toMatchObject({ id: a.organizationId });
 180: 
 181:       // (6) AND THE UNFILTERED LISTING, which is a DIFFERENT attack from a keyed probe: a keyed
 182:       // probe asks whether one identifier is yours, a listing asks what is there at all, and a
 183:       // policy that leaked would leak there first.
 184:       const organizations = await sut.dataApiRead(sessionB, { table: 'organizations', keyedBy: null, value: null });
 185:       expect(organizations.rows, 'B\'s unfiltered listing of organisations was refused before any row was considered').not.toBeNull();
 186:       expect(
 187:         (organizations.rows ?? []).map((row) => String(row.id)),
 188:         'B\'s unfiltered listing is not exactly its own organisation',
 189:       ).toEqual([b.organizationId]);
 190:       const projects = await sut.dataApiRead(sessionB, { table: 'projects', keyedBy: null, value: null });
 191:       expect(projects.rows, 'B\'s unfiltered listing of projects was refused before any row was considered').not.toBeNull();
 192:       expect(
 193:         (projects.rows ?? []).map((row) => String(row.id)),
 194:         'B\'s unfiltered listing shows a project B does not own',
 195:       ).toEqual([]);
 196: 
 197:       // (7) AND THE NO-ORACLE PROPERTY SURVIVES A DATABASE FAULT — gate-1 ruling 4's PROOF rather
 198:       // than its assertion, and the loop tier's own arm. `_live.ts` does not back `failNextReadOf`,
 199:       // so there is no fault injection at the integration tier and an integration body reaching for
 200:       // it would refuse by name rather than fault a real database.
 201:       //
 202:       // THIS IS THE SURFACE THAT MOST NEEDS THE ARM, and that is why it is here as well as on the
 203:       // project workspace. `organization-dashboard` makes FOUR reads and THREE of them precede the
 204:       // target, so it is where a lookup issued AFTER the target read would first appear. Such a
 205:       // fault is reachable only on the path where the target EXISTS: a real foreign organisation
 206:       // would answer 502 while an identifier that names nothing had already answered 404, and those
 207:       // two answers are an existence oracle sitting outside `TENANT_NOT_FOUND`. With every decision
 208:       // read issued BEFORE the target read and nothing after it, both answer the same 502 — by
 209:       // construction rather than by care.
 210:       //
 211:       // ALL FOUR STORES, THE TARGET'S OWN INCLUDED. A fault on `organizations` faults the target
 212:       // read itself, and that answer must be identical for both identifiers too; asserting it is
 213:       // what makes this a complete statement rather than one about the first three reads only. The
 214:       // fault is ONE-SHOT — it is consumed by the read it fails — so each call arms its own.
 215:       for (const store of ['accounts', 'memberships', 'projects', 'organizations'] as const) {
 216:         await sut.failNextReadOf(store);
 217:         const faultedForeign = await sut.organizationDashboard(sessionB, a.organizationId);
 218:         await sut.failNextReadOf(store);
 219:         const faultedAbsent = await sut.organizationDashboard(sessionB, ABSENT_ID);
 220:         expect(faultedForeign.ok, `a faulted ${store} read answered with a projection`).toBe(false);
 221:         expect(faultedForeign.status, `a faulted ${store} read did not answer as an outage`).toBe(502);
 222:         expect(
 223:           faultedAbsent,
 224:           `under a fault on ${store} a real foreign organisation and one that names nothing answered differently`,
 225:         ).toEqual(faultedForeign);
 226:       }
 227:     },
 228:     integration: at00121,
 229:   },
 230: );
